179 units of product 0 when 137 units of product 1 arrive, which is more than the 288 capacity. About 30–50 units of product 1 overflow, and around day 18 it is right at the limit again. Holding costs are also the highest of all plans.\n- **Candidates 0, 5 and 7** order every 3 days, so both products arrive together on the same days.\n  - Candidate 7 brings 180 + 106 = 286 units on day 14 plus leftovers, so it overflows almost certainly.\n  - Candidate 5 brings 175 + 97 = 272 plus about 15–30 leftover units, which is right at the limit. I expect about 15 units of product 1 to be lost, worth roughly 150–200.\n  - Average stock is about one day of demand, against half a day for a 2-day cycle. That costs about 175 more in holding.\n- **Candidates 2 and 6** order every 2 days: 14 × 48 = 672. Product 0 arrives on odd days and product 1 on even days, so they never land together. The largest single arrival is 149–162 units, so capacity is never at risk, and holding is the lowest of the cyclic plans.\n\n**Comparison.**\n- Candidate 2 vs 5: quantities are almost identical. Candidate 2 pays about 144 more in fees but saves about 175 in holding and avoids the overflow, so it comes out roughly 150–250 ahead.\n- Candidate 2 vs 6: candidate 6 buys about 94 extra units of product 0. They prevent only about 25 shortages (worth about 600) and cost 940 to buy, so candidate 6 is worse.\n- Candidate 2 vs 0: candidate 0 has too little of both products. It loses about 300–500 to shortages.\n- Candidates 1, 4, 3 and 7 trail further behind.\n\n**Under randomness**, candidate 2 is the sturdiest of the cheap plans: it has no capacity risk and a moderate surplus. Candidate 5 is close behind, with candidate 6 after it.\n\nMy confidence that candidate 2 is actually best is about 0.4."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 3870, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2304, "cache_creation": {"epheme